      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around Without a Car line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I feel good with you.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Мне тоже хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I feel good too.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'too' directly with 'good', combining 'too' with 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Особенно когда ты без одежды.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Especially when you are without clothes.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'especially' directly with 'when', combining 'especially' with 'when' and 'clothes'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты очень смешная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are very funny.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'you' directly with 'funny'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я серьезно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am serious.' Sasha delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'seriously'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I know.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'know'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Тебе вызвать такси?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Should I call you a taxi?' Sasha turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'to call', followed by 'taxi'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Max asks this with a single word meaning 'what for', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Ты сегодня без машины.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are without a car today.' Sasha uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'cars', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Как ты будешь добираться домой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How will you get home?' Here Sasha anchors the line on the word meaning 'how' and builds the thought around 'will' and 'get to'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я поеду на метро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will go by subway.' Max uses the perfective verb meaning 'will go' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'metro'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Метро уже закрыто.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The subway is already closed.' Sasha expresses this naturally by combining the word for 'metro' with 'already' and 'to close'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
